[00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:50.800]   and protecting
[00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:51.800]   the safety
[00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:52.800]   and health
[00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:53.800]   of our team members.
[00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:54.800]   Corn prices
[00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:55.800]   have been rising
[00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:56.800]   since August,
[00:17:56.800 --> 00:17:57.800]   driven higher
[00:17:57.800 --> 00:17:58.800]   by a combination
[00:17:58.800 --> 00:17:59.800]   of stronger-than-expected
[00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:00.800]   export demand
[00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:01.800]   and smaller-than-expected
[00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:02.800]   crop ending stocks
[00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:03.800]   in the U.S.
[00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:04.800]   USDA is projecting
[00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:05.800]   new crop ending stocks
[00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:06.800]   at 2.17 billion bushels
[00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:07.800]   versus 1.99 billion
[00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:08.800]   last year,
[00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:09.800]   which includes
[00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:10.800]   a 550 million bushels
[00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:11.800]   increase
[00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:12.800]   in export demand.
[00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:13.800]   The current corn crop
[00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:14.800]   is projected
[00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:15.800]   at 14.7 billion bushels,
[00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:16.800]   the highest
[00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:17.800]   in four years,
[00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:18.800]   and over
[00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:19.800]   1 billion bushels
[00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:20.800]   larger
[00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:21.800]   than last year.
[00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:22.800]   Although corn prices
[00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:23.800]   have risen
[00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:24.800]   from the lows
[00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:25.800]   we saw in August,
[00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:26.800]   prices are very similar
[00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:27.800]   to where they were
[00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:28.800]   last year.
[00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:29.800]   We expect
[00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:30.800]   to see
[00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:31.800]   more growth
[00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:32.800]   in the market
[00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:33.800]   this time
[00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:34.800]   last year.
[00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:35.800]   Soybean meal
[00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:36.800]   prices have also
[00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:37.800]   risen
[00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:38.800]   since August,
[00:18:38.800 --> 00:18:39.800]   driven higher
[00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:40.800]   by larger-than-expected
[00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:41.800]   export demand,
[00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:42.800]   primarily
[00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:43.800]   to China.
[00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:44.800]   USDA is projecting
[00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:45.800]   the current
[00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:46.800]   soybean crop
[00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:47.800]   at 4.3 billion bushels,
[00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:48.800]   up over
[00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:49.800]   700 million
[00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:50.800]   from last year.
[00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:51.800]   Despite
[00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:52.800]   the large increase
[00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:53.800]   in production,
[00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:54.800]   USDA is projecting
[00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:55.800]   new crop ending stocks
[00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:56.800]   at 290 million bushels,
[00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:57.800]   the lowest level
[00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:58.800]   in four years.
[00:18:58.800 --> 00:18:59.800]   The uncertainty
[00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:00.800]   over the size
[00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:01.800]   of the Chinese
[00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:02.800]   import program
[00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:03.800]   is causing
[00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:04.800]   increasing
[00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:05.800]   uncertainty
[00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:06.800]   and volatility
[00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:07.800]   in the oil
[00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:08.800]   seeds market globally.
[00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:09.800]   Wheat prices
[00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:10.800]   in Europe
[00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:11.800]   have also risen
[00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:12.800]   recently despite
[00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:13.800]   the larger-than-expected
[00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:14.800]   Russian wheat crop
[00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:15.800]   and projected
[00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:16.800]   increased supply
[00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:17.800]   in other major
[00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:18.800]   wheat exporters
[00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:19.800]   like Australia.
[00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:20.800]   A slow start
[00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:21.800]   to the planting
[00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:22.800]   season in Russia
[00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:23.800]   and larger-than-expected
[00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:24.800]   export demand
[00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:25.800]   out of the U.S.
[00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:26.800]   are contributing
[00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:27.800]   to the rise
[00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:28.800]   prices
[00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:29.800]   in the wheat market.
[00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:30.800]   As a result
